            <ArticleTitle>Total hip arthroplasty for late hip dislocation in paraplegia.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>338-341</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.5301/HIP.2008.2551</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Late hip dislocation is uncommon, particularly in the context of paraplegia. We report a case in which total hip arthroplasty with a semi-constrained acetabular component was a successful treatment for this condition. A review of the literature revealed that this method of treatment had not been previously described in paraplegics. For patients with late hip dislocation in spastic paraplegia, total hip arthroplasty with a semi-constrained acetabular component, combined with adequate adductor release and obturator neurectomy is recommended. (Hip International 2002; 12: 338-41).</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
